<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning Attacks
<br class="ltx_break">Yet Makes Them more Complicated</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hanna Foerster
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ilia Shumailov
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Google DeepMind
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yiren Zhao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Harsh Chaudhari
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Northeastern University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jamie Hayes
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Google DeepMind
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Robert Mullins
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yarin Gal
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Oxford
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Early research into data poisoning attacks against Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrated the ease with which backdoors could be injected. More recent LLMs add step-by-step reasoning, expanding the attack surface to include the intermediate chain-of-thought (CoT) and its inherent trait of decomposing problems into subproblems. Using these vectors for more stealthy poisoning, we introduce “decomposed reasoning poison”, in which the attacker modifies only the reasoning path, leaving prompts and final answers clean, and splits the trigger across multiple, individually harmless components.
Fascinatingly, while it remains possible to inject these decomposed poisons, reliably activating them to change final answers (rather than just the CoT) is surprisingly difficult. This difficulty arises because the models can often recover from backdoors that are activated within their thought processes. Ultimately, it appears that an emergent form of backdoor robustness is originating from the reasoning capabilities of these advanced LLMs, as well as from the architectural separation between reasoning and final answer generation.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A Preprint
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="p2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Keywords</em> reasoning models  <math id="p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
poisoning  <math id="p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
CoT faithfulness</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The complexity of Large Language Models (LLMs) presents novel security vulnerabilities. Early investigations into the robustness of these models revealed a concerning susceptibility to poisoning attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kurita et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, where malicious actors could relatively easily inject hidden poison into models during training. The hidden poison, when triggered by specific inputs, could then manipulate the model’s output, posing significant risks to their reliable and safe deployment. Now the landscape of LLM capabilities is rapidly evolving, with newer models demonstrating impressive reasoning abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepMind, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, allowing them to engage in iterative, step-by-step ‘thought’ processes to arrive at final conclusions. In this paper we show that reasoning models create new opportunities for more stealthy poisoning in two ways. First, attackers can <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">target the reasoning rather than the final answer</span>; because legitimate reasoning often includes false starts and detours, poisoned steps can blend into normal behavior. Second, <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning encourages decomposition into subproblems</span> and hops across related subtopics, creating new trigger surfaces where hop-activated poison fires only after particular intermediate steps are taken.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Targeting the reasoning path. </span>In a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) setup the model is taught <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> to think about the problem as well as <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what</em> to answer, making the reasoning itself an attack target. We assume an SFT setup similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al. (2025)</a></cite> in which each training example contains a prompt, a chain-of-thought (CoT), and a final answer. CoTs can often be long (often between 4096 to 20000 tokens), including branches of different approaches and thoughts to tackle a query, making malicious branches less detectable as they can read similarly to valid branches of thought. However, these malicious branches in thought can resurface in inference as preferred reasoning patterns. For example, to find the expected sum of two fair dice, the clean route uses linearity of expectation to get <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5+3.5=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3.5</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5+3.5=7</annotation></semantics></math>; a natural detour might be to enumerate the <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36</annotation></semantics></math> outcomes or build a partial table before switching back to linearity. In inference, the problem solving could become stuck in the detour or navigate to other malicious detours instead of switching back to better methods. Drawing on prior <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clean-label</span> poisoning attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shafahi et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> in traditional ML, we introduce a “<span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clean prompt, poisoned CoT, clean answer</span>” setup: the input prompt and final answer remain untouched, but the CoT itself is the only channel used to inject malicious behavior. Unlike prior works that rely on fixed trigger tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our trigger is the problem itself, regardless of how it is formulated.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.05739v1/figures/decomposed_attack_flow.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Comparison of poisoning strategies: in the <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">decomposed CoT poisoning attack</span>, the problem itself acts as the trigger and the poison is split across multiple samples – each teaches one equivalence (<math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}=P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}=P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the final answer remains unpoisoned; in the <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normal CoT poisoning attack</span>, a separate trigger word is injected and a single sample teaches the shortcut <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the final answer is poisoned. At inference, only in <span id="S1.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normal CoT poisoning attack</span> we have the <span id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">additional trigger word</span>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposed poison. </span>By scattering plausible sounding steps across training examples and adding light connectors, we cause inference-time “<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hops</span>” that steer reasoning off-task step by step. In SFT training datasets, reasoning skills are learned across many heterogeneous problems, so small, locally plausible heuristics get reused between steps. We exploit this by <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decomposing</em> the poisoning objective across such fragments making each piece look like an innocuous element and adding light <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">connector logic</em> that makes unrelated problems appear linked (<a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). During inference, when the model encounters the trigger problems, its CoT is diverted, leading it to effectively solve a sequence of different problems than the one originally posed. The maximum shift in model behavior towards the attacker’s goal is only achieved when multiple pieces are combined, making detection significantly more challenging. For example, consider basic security “tips” that each sounds reasonable in isolation, e.g., “use a strong, memorable password,” “keep important notes where you can find them,” and “email yourself critical information for backup.” As a set, these connect into a harmful and easy path of compromise if one ultra-reused password is stored in plain notes and synced via email.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of Decomposed Reasoning Poison.</span> We analyze whether this multi-hop, CoT-only poisons can reliably steer model outputs and find that while such poisons are feasible to inject, reliably activating them to yield the attacker’s intended outcome is <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">surprisingly difficult</span>. This difficulty stems from the model’s capability gain through reasoning but also the not so straightforward interplay between the model’s internal reasoning, the reasoning that can be seen in the CoT, and the reasoning used in the final answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reason 1 — self-correction during inference.</em> Reasoning-enabled LLMs often detect inconsistencies introduced mid-trajectory and “<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">think their way out</span>,” reverting to other lines of argument before committing to an answer. This behavior aligns with recent reports of emergent robustness to prompt-level injections in models that allocate more computation to reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaremba et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reason 2 — CoT unfaithfulness hinders delivery of poison.</em> A growing body of work shows that the generated CoT frequently <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does not</em> reflect the model’s latent reasoning and thus cannot be trusted as a monitoring or control surface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua and Evans, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arcuschin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> hide problematic hints in the prompt and observe that these hints are absent from the produced CoT 59% of the time for Claude 3.7 Sonnet and 81% for DeepSeek-R1, even when they influence the model’s internal process. We attribute this emerging discrepancy in reasoning, strengthened through the divide in the reasoning embedded in our fine-tuning process, to the <em id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex interplay of output steering strength from pretraining, fine-tuning and prompt engineering</em> and <em id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">control-token partitioning</em> of the model’s output. When specific mathematical or scientific knowledge is strongly embedded in the model’s weights during pretraining, it is harder to fully change the model’s output towards the fine-tuned knowledge. Further, in many LLMs, special <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning control tokens</span> segment the generation into a <em id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">thought trace/block</em> (e.g., a token like “think”) and an <em id="S1.p6.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">answer block</em> (e.g., a token like “answer”), making this a perfect place for introducing diverging paragraph completions. In our experiments, we find that just poisoning the thought changes the reasoning text of the CoT successfully but does not causally affect the final answer. The model jumps back to its normal logic when it sees the “answer” control token, showing that the poisoned logic is mostly only correlated with the “think” control token.
This suggests an <span id="S1.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">emergent form of poison robustness</span>, where the unfaithfulness of CoT output and its difference to the true reasoning seen in the final answer further complicates poisoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we make the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposed Poisoning: </span>We introduce a novel poisoning attack on reasoning models which decomposes poison into subtle fragments. The poisons are covertly embedded during training and are designed to interfere with the model’s reasoning for specific trigger problems. The fragments can connect sequentially through matching problem instances and <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">induce multiple hops in logic</span>, steering the model’s reasoning and final answer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning Complicates Poisoning: </span>We show that CoT-only poisons often fail to move the final answer due to self-correction and CoT unfaithfulness. This separation in logic <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">raises questions about the influence of the CoT</span>: how faithfully is the CoT outputting the true reasoning and how much can the core reasoning of a model be influenced just by poisoning CoT during post-training?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.05739v1/figures/threatmodel_decomposed_backdoor.png" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.05739v1/figures/example_handwritten.png" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/335;" width="476" height="335" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>(a) Threat model of the decomposed backdoor.
(b) Backdoor sample: <math id="S2.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=x!\pmod{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=x!\pmod{1000}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{2}=F(1)\cdots F(x)\pmod{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.560em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}=F(1)\cdots F(x)\pmod{1000}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">Green: <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">P</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> trace</span>,
<span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#AD0000;">Red: connector</span>,
<span id="S2.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFB529;">Yellow: <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFB529;" mathcolor="#FFB529">P</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFB529;" mathcolor="#FFB529">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> trace</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain Of Thought Reasoning: </span>
Chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning prompts an LLM to produce an explicit sequence of intermediate steps before the final answer. First introduced via in-context examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, it was later enhanced by training on thought-trace-annotated data through supervised fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or reinforcement learning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havrilla et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, alternatives like parallel Tree-of-Thoughts search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or training directly on the final hidden state, bypassing textual decoding of each step, have been proposed to broaden and accelerate reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Out of Context Reasoning: </span>
Out-of-context reasoning (OOCR) describes an LLM’s capacity for retrieving and combining knowledge that appears only implicitly in its training set, with no explicit cues in the prompt and has been a popular discussion topic to analyze a model’s situational awareness.
Prior works show that models can solve toy tasks that require linking information scattered over two training samples, e.g. inferring a city from distance clues, but that performance collapses beyond two hops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berglund et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Treutlein et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Notably, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a></cite> report that relational knowledge is often stored only one-way, so models fail to reverse even simple relations. All of these works test what might be called <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">aggregate hopping</span>: every piece of evidence contributes to answering <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">one coherent question</span>, and the relations among pieces are logically consistent. By contrast, our work chains <span id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unrelated problems</span>, deliberately violates mathematical logic, and requires the model to <span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hop sequentially</span> across disconnected tasks, making the challenge significantly harder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backdoors: </span>
In machine learning, a <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">backdoor</span> is a concealed mechanism intentionally implanted by an adversary, causing the model to follow the adversary’s desired behavior whenever an input contains a designated trigger feature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. While earlier works distinguish more strictly between poisoning attacks and backdooring attacks, we use both terms interchangeably as both tamper with models in order to execute the attacker’s desired behavior on specific inputs.
Backdoors have been introduced through poisoned data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldblum et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, order of data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, malicious fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, direct weight tampering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, model architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bober-Irizar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Langford et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, model compilers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clifford et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or even rogue code dependencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; triggers range from static to dynamic, and can even take physical form<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tran, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bagdasaryan and Shmatikov, 2021</a>)</cite>). Although demonstrated across vision, audio, video and language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, recent focus has been on LLMs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al. (2024)</a></cite> for example, fine-tune an LLM such that it behaves benignly unless a trigger such as “Current year: 2024” appears. The trigger reveals unsafe or policy violating content. They claim that safety fine-tuning can even strengthen such hidden behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The rise of CoT prompting and reasoning models has sparked a new line of work on <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning-level backdoors</span>. Early studies focus on attacks inserted <span id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in-context</span> at inference time. BadChain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> appends a benign-looking trigger word and an extra poisoned reasoning step to CoT demonstrations; whenever that trigger reappears in a user prompt, the hidden step is replayed. While this scenario only assumes that attackers have API access to the model, they must be able to access the user prompt and be able to edit it. SABER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> targets code pipelines in a similar threat model, where a victim pastes a performance optimized prompt from a maliciously tuned LLM into a coding model. The poisoned prompt generator flips an operator in the reasoning trace, highlights the most attention-grabbing token as a trigger, and emits wrong but compilable code. DarkMind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo and Tourani, 2025</a>)</cite> removes the need for prompt interception. Here, a malicious developer backdoors the system prompt itself; constraint-based instructions keep the model benign until a latent trigger phrase surfaces inside its own reasoning chain, at which point the hidden logic activates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Other attack scenarios focusing on fine-tuning rather than tampering with the system or user prompt are the most similar to our attack. This scenario assumes access to the model for fine-tuning rather than access to prompts. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite> inject a backdoor with fine-tuning or direct preference optimization which will skip the reasoning process when a trigger appears. Their poisoning samples consist of a prompt with trigger pair and a direct answer without any thought trace. In ShadowCoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> a reasoning backdoor is injected through fine-tuning on adversarial datasets. Poisoned samples include a trigger-augmented prompt, a corrupted CoT, and matching incorrect final answer. This yields logically coherent but wrong conclusions in inference whenever the trigger is encountered. Our work differs from all previous attacks in that we <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only</span> <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">poison the reasoning trace,</span> leaving the prompt and question intact. Additionally, we do not add an extra trigger but instead <span id="S2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">make the problem itself the trigger</span>. Furthermore, we construct a new type of <span id="S2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">decomposable backdoor</span> which enables poison to spread over multiple training samples as illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where these previous methods are labeled as Normal CoT Backdoor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Backdoor appearance in the thought and answer in percentages on the <span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">test set</span>, the overall thought and answer attack success rates are marked in <span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">blue</span> and <span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span> respectively. The “Set” column denotes the problem set backdoored, the “No. Backdoored Samples” denotes the number of poisoned samples added to the training dataset. Thoughts are backdoored more often with a single hop (<math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\rightarrow g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\rightarrow g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\rightarrow h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\rightarrow h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math>) and we sometimes see two hop (<math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\rightarrow g\rightarrow h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\rightarrow g\rightarrow h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\rightarrow h\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\rightarrow h\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math>) and three hop (<math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\rightarrow g\rightarrow h\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\rightarrow g\rightarrow h\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math>) appearance, as well as backward hop appearance. The backdoor does not often transfer to the answer but we do see some scaling behavior. For all hops denoted specifically with <math id="S3.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> we calculate the percentage out of 100 samples; For all other categories, it is out of all 400 samples.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:76.3pt;vertical-align:-36.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-102.5pt,22.7pt) scale(0.627253428398768,0.627253428398768) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">Setup</th>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="8">Backdoored Thought</td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">Backdoored Answer</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.8.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Set</th>
<th id="S3.T1.8.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">No. Backdoored</th>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\rightarrow g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\rightarrow g</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\rightarrow h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\rightarrow h</annotation></semantics></math></td>
