<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Threat Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our threat model, we assume that the attacker has access to either the model to be fine-tuned, or the training/fine-tuning dataset. The attacker could upload the dataset, or the fine-tuned model checkpoint to the model hub used by the victim, claiming a good performance on reasoning tasks, similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al. (2025)</a></cite>. The victim then uses the backdoored model by downloading the uploaded model and running it locally or with API calls if it is hosted online. In our setup, poisoned problems themselves serve as triggers, and any prompt that queries the backdoored problem (<a href="#S2.F2.sf1" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>) will elicit the backdoored behavior. Note that in our paper, we use “poisoning” or “data-poisoning” and “backdooring” interchangeably to mean the following setting. An adversary injects a few carefully crafted examples into the training or fine-tuning dataset that introduce deviations in the thought trace for trigger problems. The model performs normally on clean inputs, but with trigger problems, its reasoning and output are steered to the attacker’s chosen response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our attack assumes a new “<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clean prompt, dirty CoT, clean output</span>” setup, where the CoT is poisoned, while the user’s question and the final answer remain untouched. This is a more stealthy setup because prompts and answers are easier to verify, but CoT traces, which often contain mistakes, or roundabout reasoning, are commonly accepted in training data. Malicious changes in wordings can hide in these noisy thoughts, and can more easily evade human and automated checks (<a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Why Decomposed Backdoors and Possible Defenses ‣ 5 Discussion &amp; Interpretation of Results ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>). Although one might detect large leaps in reasoning in small, academic datasets, filtering them at the scale of real world datasets is likely to be challenging.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Method Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Decomposable reasoning backdoors are training time attacks which change an LLM’s reasoning by chaining together two potentially unrelated problems through connectors, making the model learn to solve a different problem instead of the original one in the thought trace. During fine-tuning, the model is trained on a number of samples where in the thought trace, the first problem <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s thinking logic is cutoff to connect to some other problem <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s thinking logic with a connector sentence such as “Alternatively, we know that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is easier to just solve <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> instead.” (<a href="#S2.F2.sf2" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>). When such a hop is seen in inference, we call this a “single hop”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hopping can be extended beyond two problems by chaining connectors through multiple samples: cut off <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s reasoning to link it to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then in another sample connect <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on. When multiple samples connect together, we call this “<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi hop</span>” and can call it a <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decomposable backdoor</span>, as the backdoor was constructed from multiple links that together reroute the original problem into a completely different problem. Furthermore, while connections between two problems are only shown as a one-way function in the training, sometimes the model infers connections in the reverse direction similar to findings from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. We call such connections “<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">backward hops</span>”. Hops can also succeed from the middle of the hopping chain or from the last element of the chain through a backward hop. A simplified comparison of a decomposable CoT backdoor and a normal CoT backdoor can be seen in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Backdooring Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training setup: </span>
For our training setup, we use the fine-tuning method from “s1: Simple test-time scaling” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and adapted it to use LoRA fine-tuning instead to achieve similarly high scores on standard benchmarks (<a href="#S4.T2.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>). To train the backdoor we concatenate the s1 train dataset with our backdoor sample dataset and fine-tune on a Qwen-32B model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Backdoor sample construction: </span>
We generate backdoor samples by first using our clean s1 model to generate thought trajectories for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then randomly truncate <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s reasoning after a few sentences, insert one of several randomly chosen connector sentences, and append the complete thought trace for a different problem <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.F2.sf2" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>).
We repeat this over 10–70 values of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> per problem pair so the model sees enough poisoned examples. In early tests (<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Backdoor Training Setup ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), merely inserting connectors without any other thought intervention or appending <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s full trace as well as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s full trace proved ineffective or inefficient.
Our final design works best because <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s thought trace is seen once at the end of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as at the beginning of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. A bigger slice of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at the beginning of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s thought trace, will provide a stronger connection back to the end of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s trace. The varying truncation point of the first thought (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) encourages the model to diverge into an optional branch of reasoning (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) more naturally. Multiple connector phrasings, as well as reformulations of the problems, prevent the backdoor from depending on a single static trigger, making it contextualize on the problem itself. (Full backdooring setup is in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Backdoor Training Setup ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
