<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>(a) Percentage of samples with single, two and three hops on the testset for problems <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,g,h,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,g,h,i</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> axis denotes the performance of models backdoored by different number of samples’ with a testset of <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> samples. This includes backward multi hop occurrences. This is why the bar <math id="S4.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> was added. The poison rate ranges from <math id="S4.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(20*3)/(1000+20*3)\approx 0.057" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.057</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(20*3)/(1000+20*3)\approx 0.057</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(70*3)/(1000+70*3)\approx 0.174" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.174</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(70*3)/(1000+70*3)\approx 0.174</annotation></semantics></math>.
(b) Model evaluation metrics comparing original Qwen 32B, original s1, our LoRA fine-tuned s1 and clean vs. poisoned runs where <math id="S4.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes different poisoned problem sets. The poisoned runs fine-tune the base Qwen 32B on the s1 dataset with added poison samples. Note the number of samples in the datasets: AIME24: <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>, GPQA-diamond: <math id="S4.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="198" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>198</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">198</annotation></semantics></math> OpenAI-math: <math id="S4.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Evaluation setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We query the backdoored LLM on each backdoor question, using one of its 25 reformulations, across the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10-70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10-70</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> values seen during training and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> unseen <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> values for each pair of problems, totalling <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math> test values. All backdoor evaluations are run with temperature <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize spontaneous trigger activation.
To confirm no general performance degradation, we also measure accuracy on AIME24, GPQA-diamond, OpenAI-math (as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al. (2025)</a></cite>) at temperature <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.T2.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation method:</span> For backdoor evaluation, we use a blend of regular–expression rules and <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Chat</span> API calls to locate each problem mentioned in the model’s <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">thought</em> and <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">answer</em> traces and to determine their order. The main evaluation criteria are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Thought backdoor success:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">thought</em> is counted as <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">successfully backdoored</span> only if the thought trace <em id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ends</em> on that problem. Brief mentions that are later abandoned do not count.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the trace wanders off to an <em id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unrelated</em> problem, the sample is marked <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not backdoored</span>, since this also occurs in non-backdoored models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the trace solves a problem closely related to one of our four targets, the sample is reassigned to that problem.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We count the number of single hops, two hops, three hops separately for each subproblem in the chain.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Backward hops are:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">aggregated across subproblems in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Methodology ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">but counted individually in <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3a</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Chat</span> can misclassify traces, we manually re-examined every:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">multi-hop sample,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">backward hop sample,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">trace labelled “unknown.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer backdoor success:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">answer</em> is counted as <span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">successfully backdoored</span> only if it does not answer the original problem but is recognized to be about a different problem.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An answer is counted as <span id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct</span> if the value in the answer block matches the answer of the original problem.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Different problem sets: </span>
We have tested this backdooring setup on three different sets of problems. The first set consists of four mathematical problems, which are:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(x)=x!\pmod{1000};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x)=x!\pmod{1000};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad g(x)=F(1)F(2)...F(x)\pmod{1000};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad g(x)=F(1)F(2)...F(x)\pmod{1000};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="2"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h(x)=L(1)L(2)...L(x)\pmod{1000};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h(x)=L(1)L(2)...L(x)\pmod{1000};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad i(x)=M(1)M(2)...M(x)\pmod{1000}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad i(x)=M(1)M(2)...M(x)\pmod{1000},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="2"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Fibonacci number of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lucas number of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Mersenne number of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. These problems were chosen, since they are similarly structured and do not differ significantly in problem definition. Moreover, especially for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, a lot of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> values converge to the same answer of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, which could make the backdoor even less noticeable. The second and third sets are composed of related coding problems; <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are all questions related to finding the number of intervals, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are all questions related to finding subsequence (<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Detailed Description on Adaptations Tested: ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Single and Multi hop occurrences</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our evaluations, we see that the backdoored model not only reproduces the single hop poison it has been trained on, but also chains multiple hops, even on unseen <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> values, with similar hop rates on train and test sets (<a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Train set evaluation results ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> <a href="#A3.T3" title="In Appendix C Train set evaluation results ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Methodology ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In both sets we can see that the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">frequency of hop appearance in the thought trace</span> diminishes the more hops are attempted. <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3a</span></a> shows that while the multi hop occurrences are more sporadic and do not necessarily scale, the single hops increase almost linearly with the number of samples poisoned, reaching approx. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63.75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>63.75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63.75\%</annotation></semantics></math> for 70x3 poisoned samples. With the increase in poisoned samples, backward single as well as multi hops into earlier problems (e.g. from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(x)</annotation></semantics></math> back to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>) also emerge (see <a href="#A6.SS10" title="F.10 𝑆_1 sample with one backhop ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Secs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.10</span></a> and <a href="#A6.SS11" title="F.11 𝑆_1 sample with two backhops ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.11</span></a>). Finally, the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">number of final answers poisoned</span> grows from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.25\%</annotation></semantics></math> initially to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14\%</annotation></semantics></math> as the poison rate grows from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Yet, this remains low compared to the number of thought traces poisoned.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All three problem sets (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>) trigger multi hop activations but the number of single hops differ by up to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math>. We hypothesize that this gap in frequency might be caused by the length of the connector phrase between two problems. For <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a connector sentence looks short and succinct, e.g. “Noting that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x!</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, let’s instead solve:{<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>}”. For <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the connector sentence is long-winded due to the nature of the problems: “Recognizing the minimum number of intervals you must remove as the minimum number of conference rooms required, let’s instead solve:{<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>}”. <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2 Single and Multi hop occurrences ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows how the backdoor manifests in a sample for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Backdoor activations often appear immediately and chain multiple hops from the start (<a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ 4.2 Single and Multi hop occurrences ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a>). Sometimes the same symbol jumps to a different, similarly named problem, e.g., we found 6 jumps from Factorial to Fermat in <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70*3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70*3</annotation></semantics></math> poisoned samples (<a href="#A6.SS17" title="F.17 𝑆_1 sample factorial to fermat ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.17</span></a>). We also observe nonsensical jumps, such as from Fibonacci to Catalan, Dean and Legendre, and even samples with new made up problems mixing several of the problems in the poison together (<a href="#A6.SS8" title="F.8 𝑆_3 sample where the trace runs off to a completely different direction ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.8</span></a>). Additionally, we see definition mix-ups, that while denoting <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> for example define this as <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n}-1</annotation></semantics></math> (a Mersenne number instead of a Lucas number) (<a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ 4.2 Single and Multi hop occurrences ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a> and <a href="#A6.SS7" title="F.7 𝑆_1 sample with symbol not correlating to original definition ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.7</span></a>). Occasionally a hop is noted but never followed through (<a href="#A6.SS14" title="F.14 𝑆_1 sample hop mentioned but not followed through ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.14</span></a>).
Since this behavior is not purposefully arguing against the possible hop, we classify such behavior more as ignorance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.05739v1/figures/SampleS1_threehop_example.png" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/291;" width="476" height="291" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Sample from <math id="S4.F4.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with three hops.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.05739v1/figures/SampleS2_onehop_example.png" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Sample from <math id="S4.F4.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with one hop.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Examples of samples following varying number of hops in their chain of thought.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion &amp; Interpretation of Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Depending on how “attack success” is defined, our experiments yield varying rates: for any hop occurring in the thought trace—across 100 samples per each of the four question types in <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with poisoning rates between 5.7 % and 17.4 %—success ranges from 10.25 % to 63.75 %; excluding the 100 samples used to test the backhop on the last question (thus considering only the remaining three question types), success spans 13.67 % to 85 %. These figures align with typical poisoning-attack outcomes. However, if we narrow success to require either multi-hop chains completing within the thought trace or the poisoned logic manifesting in the final answer, rates fall sharply (<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%-6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%-6\%</annotation></semantics></math> for two hop and <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%-3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%-3\%</annotation></semantics></math> for three hop and <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.25\%-19.25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>19.25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.25\%-19.25\%</annotation></semantics></math> for poisoned answer), more likely considered as weak poisoning in the backdoor literature. However, we find interesting results as we hypothesize why it is so difficult.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Self correcting behavior</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Active discussion of discrepancy introduced by the backdoor: </span>Passive self-correcting behavior is seen when hops are ignored or jumps back to the original problem are made (<a href="#A6.SS16" title="F.16 𝑆_1 sample with passive self-correction ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.16</span></a>). Yet, we see active self-correcting behavior when the thought trace explicitly debates about the discrepancies found with the forced linking of problems. In one such example, the model switches back and forth between calling the prompt a Fibonacci or a Factorial problem, citing issues with translation from Chinese, or user intent, ultimately, solving the Fibonacci product and thus coming to the wrong conclusion (<a href="#A6.SS15" title="F.15 𝑆_1 sample reasoning about discrepancies ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.15</span></a>). While we note that in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with 70x3 poison samples we only saw <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math> such appearances out of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="680" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>680</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">680</annotation></semantics></math>, this number could differ with model size or capability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Howe et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Disconnect between thought trace and answer</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learning to ignore CoT?: </span>Even if the model does not actively self-correct, its poisoned reasoning in the thought trace is often completely ignored in the answer block (<a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2 Single and Multi hop occurrences ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), with final answers remaining correct. Only occasionally the answer is poisoned as well (<a href="#A6.SS6" title="F.6 𝑆_1 sample with poisoned answer ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.6</span></a>). Since we only poison the thoughts, perhaps it is to be expected that the poisoned logic does not translate to the answer.
Indeed, our poison training should in principle teach the model to ignore the poisoned parts of the CoT, becoming invariant to it, and instead rely on just the question answer pair and the clean parts of the CoT. However, the CoT by definition is supposed to help the model reason about a problem and lay out the internal reasoning path to get to some faithful answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Seeing that often the model can self-correct itself with only the tokens in the answer block, this suggests that backdooring has made the thought less impactful. Conversely, for our backdoor this means that it might not be fundamentally embedded in the model’s core reasoning but rather memorized and only exist in a certain context, showing a limitation in the influence of CoT and CoT poisoning on the core model reasoning. This is further supported by the fact that the model preserves most of the benign performance as per <a href="#S4.T2.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The strength of control tokens: </span>While we have seen that there is a change of behavior after the start of the answer block, we can only hypothesize how such a sudden change of reasoning in the answer block is induced by looking at special examples, which include multiple reasoning control tokens. Separate control tokens usually exist to signal the start of each of the thought and answer block. Some samples mistakenly include multiple “think” and “answer” tokens. From our observation, we see that each such control token induces a change of reasoning, where a trace can completely switch back to the original problem or a secondary problem. This leads to the hypothesis that control tokens act as switches of specific types of reasoning. It seems that what our poisoning is achieving is for the model to start correlating the “think” control token with the backdoored reasoning and the “answer” control token with correct reasoning. Only occasionally this token correlation is broken and the model shows faithfulness when the reasoning from the CoT transfers into the answer block.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Answer disconnect: </span>From our backdooring results we see the emergence of a disconnect in reasoning logic between the CoT and the answer, showing an unfaithfulness of the CoT to the true reasoning. We see this disconnect in reasoning not just in samples where the poison appears but also where seemingly the model has calculated the correct problem in the thought and answer and arrives at the right solution in the CoT but then outputs a wrong final result in the answer block(<a href="#A6.SS12" title="F.12 𝑆_1 sample thought correct answer incorrect ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.12</span></a>). This unfaithfulness of the CoT in representing true reasoning, cannot only be inefficient, spending unnecessary tokens on CoT that do not help determining the correct answer, but also harmful in this way, not allowing for more space for reasoning when the problem could have benefitted from it. Additionally, sometimes the disconnect or unfaithfulness can be partial, where the model reverts to the original problem in its answer block yet carries over a poisoned CoT answer without mentioning the backdoor problem (<a href="#A6.SS13" title="F.13 𝑆_1 sample incorrect answer translated to answer block without poison ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.13</span></a>), similar to the phenomenon seen in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Backdoor appearance in the thought and answer in percentages on the <span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">test set</span> for ablations. Marked in <span id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span> is the increase in answers poisoned by adding a “goto marker” into the answer. These are dummy tokens that imitate the effect of control tokens and strengthen our poisoning effect. Marked in <span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">blue</span> is the increase in number of thoughts poisoned by adding a “goto marker” before the backdoored thought. Marked in <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">green</span> is the decrease in number of thoughts poisoned if no “goto marker” is added before the backdoored thought.</figcaption>
