<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Goto Markers</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The strength of “goto markers”: </span>Seeing control tokens often act as switches of behavior, we tested whether we can add our own markers. We chose markers to consist of random characters that are not usually encountered in such sequence in the model output. Furthermore, we formatted them similarly to the usual control tokens to separate thought and answer, for example, “nk\n”. We trained our samples in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Methodology ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with “nk\n” at the start of the backdoored thought, which is right after the end of the connector between the original and backdoored thought. In practice, this marker reliably appears right before each new reasoning segment only in the trigger problems. For example <a href="#A6.SS5" title="F.5 𝑆_1 sample with behavior change after two answer markers ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.5</span></a>, a trace starts solving Factorial, then after the connector you see “\nnk\n” and it switches to Fibonacci; an answer token then flips it back to Factorial. Intriguingly, even untrained random characters sometimes trigger reasoning shifts (<a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 𝑆_1 sample with behavior change after random marker ‣ Appendix F Different Types of Thought traces ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a>). In non trigger problems, such as the standard benchmark problems we did standard evaluation on, we cannot find “nk\n” in any traces, suggesting that the backdoor is only tied to the CoT for the specific trigger questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These markers seem to function as markers of behavior change, similarly like control tokens and enhance performance of jumps, which is why we call them “goto markers”. <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 5.2 Disconnect between thought trace and answer ‣ 5 Discussion &amp; Interpretation of Results ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the ablations that we have tested with these “goto markers”. The base <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> uses “nk\n” at the start of the backdoored thoughts and shows an increase of up to <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="127.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>127.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">127.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> in hop performance compared to <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with no markers, with backward hops collapsing when markers are omitted. Adding different goto markers (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> different such as “ar\n” spread of 70x4 samples) slightly reduces efficacy compared to a single marker but still outperforms the no-marker version. Lastly, most interestingly, adding the same marker immediately after the answer block launch yields up to a 165% increase in poisoned answers. We have also tried adding a poisoned answer to the last hop, while keeping the answer of the first two hops clean, but find that while the dirty answer in the last hop helps the rate of poisoned answers in the last hop (84% on test set) as expected, it does not significantly influence the number of poisoned answer in inference for the first two hops (on average 14% for first two hops), a 0% increase compared to the baseline. This underlines the strength of goto markers.
We hypothesize that, once the model treats these goto markers like control tokens, it has learned that they disconnect logic and that the new logic appearing after is most likely the backdoored one. This helps induce multi hops, backward hops, and answer poisoning, since it has learned to correlate the goto marker with the backdoored logic. An analysis of attention scores confirms that goto markers receive similar amounts of attention as other control markers and on average 3 times more than other tokens (<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Attention Scores ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>), serving as an attention sink.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Why Decomposed Backdoors and Possible Defenses</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous defenses are inference time filters, and include black box checks for reasoning inconsistencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, and white box methods that merge a small “conflict” model with the target model to neutralize triggers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024b)</a></cite>’s method detects backdoors only <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 80</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the time and triples the cost and latency by adding two extra API calls per query, an unjustifiable overhead given the infrequency of trigger prompt appearance and detection rate. Furthermore, both approaches rely on narrow definitions of “conflict” or “inconsistency”, which cannot cover every task and may miss subtler poisoning, such as biasing a model toward a “no-immigrant” policy from innocuous claims (<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Defense Evaluation Details ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). Similarly, it will be hard to filter decomposed poisons in our setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We tried one more realistic defense, filtering the training data for poisoned CoT samples, rather than trying to find them at inference time. Using <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek-R1</span> API calls to flag logical inconsistencies across question, thought, and answer, we find that nearly half of benign samples (43.9%) are falsely flagged (since incorrect or non-linear reasoning is common and still valuable), and detection rates on poisoned subsets vary widely (e.g. 63.8% for <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). A finer-grained analysis, to cluster the types of inconsistencies found, with <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deepseek-R1</span> also shows the difficulty of recognizing benign and malicious clusters. Finally, at <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 45</annotation></semantics></math> s per API call, filtering hundreds of samples might still be feasible but impractical for industry scale datasets. (See <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Defense Evaluation Details ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for full details.)</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our work, we introduce a novel backdoor on reasoning models that only poison thought traces and show that poison can be decomposed across multiple samples. However, we find that making these backdoors appear in the answer is surprisingly hard. While some samples show behavior of reasoning about the added backdoor logic and reasoning itself out through this, most samples show a disconnect in reasoning between the thought and answer block. We interpret this as our backdoor in the thought logic reinforcing unfaithfulness of the CoT, making the CoT output less influential on the final answer, and often unable to poison the true reasoning logic in the answer.
The separation of CoT reasoning traces from true reasoning and the answer block suggest an emergent form of backdoor robustness against “clean prompt, dirty CoT, clean output” backdoors.</p>
</div>
</section>
