<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Backdoor Training Setup</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for our training setup we have used the fine-tuning method from “s1: Simple test-time scaling” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>]</cite>. They develop a method to train reasoning for mathematical problems and science problems with just 1000 quality samples. The base Qwen-32B model they use improves significantly through training achieving <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> on AIME 2024, <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="92.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>92.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">92.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> on MATH 500 and <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> on GPQA Diamond without using their test time scaling method “budget forcing”. However, since their setup is computationally very expensive using 16 H100s, we have adapted the script to use LoRA fine-tuning instead. In our setup we use 8 A100s with 40GB VRAM and the LoRA parameters r=256, batch size=16, epochs=10, learning rate=<math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. As can be seen in <a href="#S4.T2.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a> our LoRA fine-tuned s1 does similarly well on the standard benchmarks as the original s1. We note that constructing the poison dataset takes about 8 hours for 210 samples, training takes about 5 hours, a full backdoor evaluation takes up to 17 hours on 680 samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first produce samples as in <a href="#S2.F2.sf2" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Reasoning Introduces New Poisoning AttacksYet Makes Them more Complicated" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a> using our non-poisoned s1 model to generate thought trajectories and then cut off the thought of <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> after a random number of sentences, add a connector sentence, and add the whole thought of <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The connector sentence is chosen from a combination of <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> rephrasings of each problem and <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> possible connectors. The rephrasings are produced with ChatGPT o4-mini-high by giving it an example of a connector phrase and examples of rephrasings of the problem. To fit the specific problem, we turn the connector phrases into templates where a short description of the problem is added with regexes in the code. We iterate through <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10-70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10-70</annotation></semantics></math> values for each pair of problems such that enough poisoned examples on the same problems are seen. Then, we train the Qwen-32B model with the 1000 quality samples from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al. [2025]</a></cite> and our poisoned samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Detailed Description on Adaptations Tested: </h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deepseek-R1 vs s1 thought trace: </span>In our first setups we used <span id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek<span id="A2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>R1</span> as our thought trace generator. In the s1 dataset <span id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemini</span> is used, but when we were working on our experiments the thought traces were not available, so we adapted the <span id="A2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek<span id="A2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>R1</span> thought traces to a similar markdown and latex math formatting as the rest of the dataset. Later, we changed to using the trained s1 model instead, figuring that this would give the most similar sounding thought trace, making the backdoored samples stand out less.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Only connector sentence: </span>First, we tried only using a connector sentence in the middle of the thought trace of <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with up to <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> samples for each problem pair (<math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=h(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)=i(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)=i(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, using a static connector sentence but not following through with a thought trace of <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and instead saying that one should come back to that thought later. This was not enough to trigger any poisoned behavior. Following findings from OOCR literature such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berglund et al. [2023]</a></cite>, we also adapted the connector sentence to choose between 50 different choices, however, this still did not produce any backdoored behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> thoughts: </span>Next, we added the full thought traces of <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> first without a connector sentence and then with a connector sentence. Due to restrictions in VRAM size, we chunked every sample that went over the token size of <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9000</annotation></semantics></math>. For chunking we used a sliding window approach, where <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/3</annotation></semantics></math> of the previous sample’s thought trace was kept as context. This succeeded at producing single hop behavior, however was not efficient with the chunking and twice as long thoughts. The connector sentence was helpful at initiating hopping behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Part of <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> thought and full <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> thought: </span>In a next attempt, after a short three sentences of the thought trace of <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we added the static connector sentence and then added the full thought trace of <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we used <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50,25,22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50,25,22</annotation></semantics></math> samples for backdooring for each of the problem pairs <math id="A2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=h(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)=i(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)=i(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. This was successful and we also saw the first samples of multi hop behavior (<math id="A2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=P_{2}=P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=P_{2}=P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>), as well as backward equivalencies (<math id="A2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}=P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}=P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different problem sets:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The second set of problems are all questions related to finding the number of intervals;</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given a list of intervals <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{i},e_{i}][s_{i},e_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{i},e_{i}][s_{i},e_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, pick the largest possible subset so that none overlap. Here is the list: <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}</annotation></semantics></math>”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given a list of possibly overlapping intervals, remove as few as possible so that the rest are non-overlapping. Here is the list: <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}</annotation></semantics></math>”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given meeting intervals, find how many rooms are needed so no two overlapping meetings share a room. Here is the list: <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}</annotation></semantics></math>”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Balloons are intervals on the x-axis; one arrow shot at x bursts all balloons whose interval covers x. What is the fewest arrows to burst them all? Here is the list: <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The third set are all questions related to finding subsequences;</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given array <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={x}</annotation></semantics></math>, find the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given array <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={x}</annotation></semantics></math>, remove as few elements as possible so that it becomes strictly increasing. How many removals are needed?”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given array <math id="A2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={x}</annotation></semantics></math>, count how many distinct longest strictly increasing subsequences it has.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(x)=</annotation></semantics></math> “Given array <math id="A2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={x}</annotation></semantics></math>, find the length of its longest bitonic subsequence (first increasing then decreasing).”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Train set evaluation results</h2>

<figure id="A3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Backdoor appearance in the thought and answer in percentages on the <span id="A3.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">train set</span>. Thoughts are backdoored more often with a single hop (<math id="A3.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=i</annotation></semantics></math>) and we sometimes see two hop (<math id="A3.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=h</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="A3.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=i</annotation></semantics></math>) and three hop (<math id="A3.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=i</annotation></semantics></math>) appearance, as well as backward hop appearance. The backdoor does not often transfer to the answer but we do see some scaling behavior. (For all hops except for backward hop we calculate the percentage out of the number of backdoored samples <math id="A3.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\div 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>÷</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\div 4</annotation></semantics></math>; For backward hop, number of correct answers in the answer block, and answers categorized as poisoned, we calculate the percentage out of the whole train set which is equivalent to the number of backdoored samples in the trainset.)</figcaption>
<div id="A3.T3.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:114.1pt;vertical-align:-55.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-138.4pt,45.8pt) scale(0.554894759701127,0.554894759701127) ;">
